CHAPTER 2: LITERATURE REVIEW

Unintegrated Foundations

Three distinct mathematical domains each address one dimension of a CDT, but no existing synthesis formalises the closed cognitive loop.

Digital Twins and Data Assimilation. The engineering literature (Tao et al., 2019) focuses on real‑time synchronisation, model reduction, and Bayesian filtering (Evensen, 2009). Even the most advanced space‑habitat twins (Gratius et al., 2024; Leese & Olthoff, 2026) treat the twin as a mirror of a physical system, not as a cognitive feedback architecture that determines policy agendas. They lack formal closure: what the twin learns today does not automatically shape what it simulates tomorrow.

Collective Memory as a Topological Object. Persistent homology (Edelsbrunner & Harer, 2008) and information theory (Shannon, 1948; Cover & Thomas, 2006) provide tools to measure what a society remembers and forgets. Recent work on orthogonal hypersphere encodings (Hal Science Preprint, 2025) further demonstrates the formalisation of collective memory, but these models are not connected to a foresight engine.

Strategic Foresight and Measure‑Theoretic Probability. Probabilistic forecasting of rare events relies on measure theory (Billingsley, 1995) and stochastic processes. Chaos theory (Strogatz, 2015) places hard limits on predictability via Lyapunov exponents. Foresight models are initialised with historical data, but the feedback from action outcomes back to the forecasting priors is not formalised as a dynamical system.

Coordination and Distributed Optimisation. Spectral graph theory (Chung, 1997) and mechanism design (Myerson, 1991) design networks that coordinate toward socially optimal outcomes. Yet the agenda—which risks to coordinate against—is taken as given, not generated by a twin that has simulated thousands of futures.

The Gap and the Categorical Solution. No existing framework treats the CDT as a single mathematical object linking these three domains in a closed loop. Category theory (Mac Lane, 1998) formalises mappings between different structures while preserving their internal relations. It is precisely the language needed to unify memory, foresight, and coordination and to state conditions under which the loop contracts.

