CHAPTER 3: MATHEMATICAL BACKGROUND

The CDT architecture and the proofs of its properties draw upon the following bodies of mathematics. Detailed definitions are given where they are first employed.

3.1 Category Theory

Categories, functors, natural transformations; enriched categories; metric spaces as categories enriched over ([0,\infty],\ge,+); complete metric spaces; limits and colimits (Mac Lane, 1998).

3.2 Persistent Homology

Simplicial complexes and filtrations; persistence modules over a field \mathbb{K}; interleaving distance d_I and bottleneck distance d_B; stability theorem: for tame modules, d_B = d_I; the space of barcodes under d_B is complete (Edelsbrunner & Harer, 2008).

3.3 Riemannian Geometry and Information Geometry

Smooth manifolds, Riemannian metrics, geodesic distance; Fisher information metric on statistical manifolds (Amari & Nagaoka, 2000). For a parameterised family of probability densities p(x|\theta) with \theta \in \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m, the Fisher Information Matrix is

g_{ij}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{x\sim p(\cdot|\theta)}\!\left[ \frac{\partial \log p}{\partial \theta^i} \frac{\partial \log p}{\partial \theta^j} \right].

The geodesic distance d_F(\theta_1,\theta_2) on the Riemannian manifold (\Theta, g) is used to measure divergence between foresight models. If \Theta is compact and g is positive definite, the manifold is complete.

3.4 Spectral Graph Theory

Normalised Laplacian L of a directed graph; algebraic connectivity \lambda_2(L); spectral distance d_{\text{spec}}(L_1,L_2)=\|L_1-L_2\|_F (Frobenius norm). The set of such Laplacians for a fixed vertex set is a closed subset of \mathbb{R}^{n^2} and hence complete. Perturbation of \lambda_2 is controlled by Weyl’s inequality or the Davis–Kahan theorem.

3.5 Information Theory (Rate–Distortion)

Entropy, mutual information; rate–distortion function R(D); Blahut–Arimoto algorithm; convexity properties. For a memoryless source with distribution \mu and distortion measure \rho, R(D) is convex and monotone decreasing (Cover & Thomas, 2006).

3.6 Nonlinear Dynamics & Stability Theory

Contraction mappings, Banach fixed‑point theorem; Lyapunov exponents, Oseledets’ multiplicative ergodic theorem; Fréchet derivatives and linearised maps on manifolds; spectral radius (Strogatz, 2015; Arnold, 1998).

3.7 Computational Complexity

P, NP, NP‑completeness; polynomial‑time approximation schemes (PTAS); geometric set cover, \varepsilon-nets, doubling dimension (Arora & Barak, 2009; Har-Peled, 2011).

