Appendix A: Formal Category Definitions and Metric Completeness

A.1 The Memory Category \mathbf{M}

Definition A.1 (Persistence module).
A persistence module over a field \mathbb{K} is a functor from the poset (\mathbb{R},\le) to the category of \mathbb{K}-vector spaces. Every tame module \mathcal{P} (one that is pointwise finite-dimensional and has finitely many critical values) can be decomposed into interval modules, yielding a barcode – a multiset of intervals [b_i,d_i) with b_i<d_i.

Definition A.2 (Bottleneck distance).
For two barcodes B_1,B_2 the bottleneck distance is

d_B(B_1,B_2)=\inf_{\gamma}\sup_{I\in B_1} \|I-\gamma(I)\|_\infty,

where \gamma ranges over all bijections between the two multisets (allowing matching to intervals of zero length) and \|(b,d)\|_\infty=\max(|b|,|d|). The interleaving distance for tame modules coincides with d_B (Stability Theorem, Edelsbrunner & Harer 2008).

Definition A.3 (Category \mathbf{M}).
Objects are finite barcodes (tame modules) with a fixed upper bound N on the number of intervals. The set of objects is equipped with the metric d_B. The category is enriched over ([0,\infty],\ge,+) by taking d_B as the enrichment; morphisms are formal interleavings that realise the distance. Completeness:

Lemma A.4. The space (\mathbf{M}_0,d_B) of all finite barcodes is complete.

Proof. Every Cauchy sequence in bottleneck distance eventually has uniformly bounded number of finite intervals; the limit is a barcode with possibly some intervals of zero length, which can be removed. The limit belongs to the space. ∎

By restricting to objects with at most N intervals we obtain a closed, hence complete, subspace. We work with this complete metric space.

A.2 The Foresight Category \mathbf{F}

Assumption A2' (Parametric exponential family). The space of future trajectories is modelled by a regular exponential family p(x|\theta) with natural parameter \theta\in\Theta\subset\mathbb{R}^m, where \Theta is compact and convex. The Fisher information matrix

g_{ij}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{p}\!\left[\frac{\partial\log p}{\partial\theta^i}\frac{\partial\log p}{\partial\theta^j}\right]

is continuous and uniformly positive definite on \Theta: there exist constants c,C>0 such that c I\le g(\theta)\le C I (in Loewner order).

Definition A.5 (Fisher–Rao distance). The Riemannian metric g induces the geodesic distance

d_F(\theta_1,\theta_2)=\inf_{\substack{\gamma:[0,1]\to\Theta\\\gamma(0)=\theta_1,\gamma(1)=\theta_2}}\int_0^1\sqrt{g_{\gamma(t)}(\dot\gamma,\dot\gamma)}\,dt.

Because \Theta is compact and g is smooth, the infimum is attained and the metric space (\Theta,d_F) is complete. The Euclidean metric on \Theta is equivalent to d_F: for any \theta_1,\theta_2,

\sqrt{c}\,\|\theta_1-\theta_2\| \le d_F(\theta_1,\theta_2) \le \sqrt{C}\,\|\theta_1-\theta_2\|.
\tag{A.1}

Definition A.6 (Category \mathbf{F}). Objects are points \theta\in\Theta (representing a forecast). The hom-set distance is d_F; morphisms are smooth paths (for enrichment only). Completeness is inherited from the compact Riemannian manifold.

A.3 The Coordination Category \mathbf{C}

Definition A.7 (Normalised Laplacian). For a strongly connected directed graph on vertex set V (|V|=n) with weighted adjacency matrix W and out-degree matrix D, the normalised Laplacian is L=I-D^{-1/2}WD^{-1/2}. We require L\mathbf{1}=0 and all non-zero eigenvalues have positive real part. The set of all such Laplacians is a closed subset of \mathbb{R}^{n\times n} (as a subset of symmetric matrices when we consider the underlying undirected skeleton; we restrict to symmetric Laplacians for simplicity). The metric is the Frobenius norm d_{\text{spec}}(L_1,L_2)=\|L_1-L_2\|_F.

Lemma A.8. The space of symmetric normalised Laplacians of strongly connected graphs on n vertices is complete under d_{\text{spec}}.

Proof. The set is a closed subset of the complete space \mathbb{R}^{n\times n} with the Frobenius norm, hence complete. ∎

We define \mathbf{C} as this complete metric space.

A.4 Product State Space \mathcal{S}

The CDT state is a triplet s=(M,\theta,L)\in\mathbf{M}\times\mathbf{F}\times\mathbf{C}. We equip \mathcal{S} with the metric

d_{\mathcal{S}}(s,s')=\max\{d_B(M,M'),\, d_F(\theta,\theta'),\, d_{\text{spec}}(L,L')\}.

Theorem A.9 (Completeness of \mathcal{S}). \mathcal{S} is a complete metric space.

Proof. The product of finitely many complete metric spaces with the maximum metric is complete. Each factor is complete (Lemmas A.4, A.8 and the compactness of \Theta). Hence \mathcal{S} is complete. ∎

