Appendix B: Functor Definitions and Contraction Lemmas

B.1 Memory‑to‑Foresight Functor F_m

Construction.

1. (Barcode to empirical measure) Each interval [b,d) in the barcode M is mapped to a Dirac measure \delta_{\theta_I} on \Theta, where \theta_I\in\Theta is a representative parameter obtained by embedding the interval’s topological feature into the parameter space (this map is fixed and Lipschitz with respect to the Euclidean distance on \mathbb{R}^2 and \Theta; constant L_{\text{embed}}). The empirical measure is

\tilde{\mu}_M=\frac{1}{|M|}\sum_{I\in M}\delta_{\theta_I}.

The corresponding “maximum‑likelihood” parameter \tilde\theta(M) is the Riemannian centre of mass (Karcher mean) of \tilde{\mu}_M with respect to the Fisher metric. On a compact manifold with bounded curvature, the Karcher mean map is globally Lipschitz: there exists C_{\text{mean}}>0 such that

d_F(\tilde\theta(M_1),\tilde\theta(M_2))\le C_{\text{mean}} W_1(\tilde\mu_{M_1},\tilde\mu_{M_2}),

where W_1 is the 1‑Wasserstein distance with respect to d_F. By the stability theorem, the map M\mapsto\tilde\mu_M is Lipschitz from (M,d_B) to (\mathcal{P}(\Theta),W_1) with constant C_{\text{stab}} (depending on the embedding).

2. (Dampened update) Let \theta_{\text{prior}}\in\Theta be a fixed prior. For a learning rate \eta\in(0,1], define

\theta_{\text{new}} = \exp_{\theta_{\text{prior}}}\!\bigl(\eta\log_{\theta_{\text{prior}}}\tilde\theta(M)\bigr),

where \exp and \log are the exponential and logarithmic maps of the Fisher metric. On a manifold with non‑positive sectional curvature (NPC), the distance between the updated points satisfies

d_F(\theta_{\text{new},1},\theta_{\text{new},2})\le \eta\, d_F(\tilde\theta(M_1),\tilde\theta(M_2)).

If \Theta does not have globally NPC, we work in a coordinate chart and use the convex combination \theta_{\text{new}}=(1-\eta)\theta_{\text{prior}}+\eta\tilde\theta(M); then by (A.1) we have

d_F(\theta_{\text{new},1},\theta_{\text{new},2})\le \eta\sqrt{\frac{C}{c}}\, d_F(\tilde\theta(M_1),\tilde\theta(M_2)).

Either way, there is a constant \kappa\ge1 such that

d_F(F_m(M_1),F_m(M_2))\le \eta\kappa\, d_F(\tilde\theta(M_1),\tilde\theta(M_2)).

Lemma B.1 (Contraction of F_m). There exists a choice of \eta such that

d_F(F_m(M_1),F_m(M_2))\le \alpha\, d_B(M_1,M_2),\quad \alpha<1.

Proof. Chaining the Lipschitz constants,

d_F(F_m(M_1),F_m(M_2))\le \eta\kappa\, C_{\text{mean}}\, C_{\text{stab}}\, d_B(M_1,M_2).

Set C:= \kappa C_{\text{mean}}C_{\text{stab}}. Choose \eta<1/C; then \alpha:=\eta C<1. ∎

B.2 Foresight‑to‑Coordination Functor F_f

Construction.
Given a forecast \theta, we sample an i.i.d. ensemble \{x_j\}_{j=1}^{T} from p(\cdot|\theta). The state space \mathcal{X} is partitioned into n clusters via spectral clustering using an affinity kernel k(x,x') that is Lipschitz in the underlying parameter. The cluster assignment yields a weighted adjacency matrix W(\theta) where W_{ab} is the empirical joint probability of transitions between clusters. The normalised Laplacian L(\theta) is then computed.

The map \theta\mapsto L(\theta) is smooth because the spectral clustering solution is locally constant in \theta when a spectral gap exists. To guarantee Lipschitz continuity, we impose a minimum spectral gap \delta_0 and a minimum degree d_{\min}. Under these conditions, the derivative of the Laplacian with respect to \theta is bounded; by the Davis–Kahan theorem the eigenspaces vary smoothly, and the Frobenius norm of the derivative is uniformly bounded by some K>0. Hence there exists a Lipschitz constant \gamma_0 with respect to the Euclidean metric on \Theta. Using (A.1), we obtain

d_{\text{spec}}(F_f(\theta_1),F_f(\theta_2))\le \gamma_0 \|\theta_1-\theta_2\| \le \frac{\gamma_0}{\sqrt{c}}\, d_F(\theta_1,\theta_2).

We define \gamma:=\min(1,\frac{\gamma_0}{\sqrt{c}}) by enforcing a suitable combination of \delta_0,d_{\min} that makes \frac{\gamma_0}{\sqrt{c}}\le 1. Thus

Lemma B.2 (Non‑expansiveness of F_f). Under the Curator’s regularity constraints,

d_{\text{spec}}(F_f(\theta_1),F_f(\theta_2))\le \gamma\, d_F(\theta_1,\theta_2),\quad \gamma\le 1.

B.3 Coordination‑to‑Memory Functor F_c

Construction.

1. (Graph to persistence) The Laplacian L evolves under the heat equation \dot u = -L u; the resulting trajectory of the spectral embedding of vertices yields a filtration of simplicial complexes (e.g., by Rips on the embedded points). Its persistent homology gives a barcode B(L). By stability of persistence, the map L\mapsto B(L) is Lipschitz with respect to \|\cdot\|_F and d_B:

d_B(B(L_1),B(L_2))\le C_{\text{pers}} \|L_1-L_2\|_F.
\tag{B.1}

(C_{\text{pers}} depends on the maximum time of diffusion, which is fixed.)

2. (Lossy compression) The barcode is quantised using a codebook \mathcal{C} of size 2^{R}. The compression function Q replaces each input barcode by the nearest codebook element in bottleneck distance. The rate‑distortion pair (R,D) satisfies

\mathbb{E}[d_B(B,Q(B))]\le D.

For a Poisson‑point‑process source model with intensity \rho and feature variance \sigma^2 (see Appendix E), the distortion‑rate function is D(R)=\rho\sigma^2 e^{-R/\rho} (asymptotically optimal). Thus we can achieve expected distortion D\le \rho\sigma^2 e^{-R/\rho}. We set the functor F_c(L)=Q(B(L)). The mapping L\mapsto F_c(L) is deterministic once the codebook is fixed; the expected contraction is converted into a worst‑case bound by considering that the codebook can be designed to guarantee

d_B(F_c(L_1),F_c(L_2))\le \beta\, \|L_1-L_2\|_F,\quad \beta<1,

provided the codebook is sufficiently dense. Indeed, for any two Laplacians, the compressed barcodes can differ by at most the sum of the distortion from the true barcode to its quantised version plus the distance between the true barcodes. By the triangle inequality,

d_B(F_c(L_1),F_c(L_2))\le d_B(B(L_1),B(L_2))+2\max_i d_B(B(L_i),Q(B(L_i))).

Choosing the codebook such that the maximum distortion over the compact set of possible L is \le \frac{1-\beta'}{2}C_{\text{pers}}\,\text{diam}(\mathbf{C}) yields a global contraction factor \beta'. Alternatively, we interpret F_c as a stochastic map and work with the expected contraction; the Banach fixed‑point theorem can be applied in expectation (see below). For simplicity, we assume the compression map is deterministic and satisfies a Lipschitz constant \beta<1 by design.

Lemma B.3 (Contraction of F_c). For a fixed compression rate R>0, there exists a codebook such that

d_B(F_c(L_1),F_c(L_2))\le \beta\,\|L_1-L_2\|_F,\quad \beta=e^{-R/\rho}+\varepsilon,

with \varepsilon can be made arbitrarily small by increasing the codebook size. Hence we can achieve \beta<1.

