Appendix C: Contraction of the Loop and Unique Fixed Point (Theorem 5.4)

Definition C.1 (Loop endofunctor).
\Phi:\mathcal{S}\to\mathcal{S} is defined by

\Phi(M,\theta,L)=\bigl(F_c(L),\,F_m(M),\,F_f(\theta)\bigr).

Lemma C.2. Suppose the Curator has chosen parameters so that

\alpha:=\eta C<1,\quad \gamma\le 1,\quad \beta<1.

Then \Phi is a contraction with constant \lambda:=\max\{\alpha,\gamma,\beta\}<1.

Proof. Take s_1=(M_1,\theta_1,L_1), s_2=(M_2,\theta_2,L_2). Then

\begin{aligned}
d_{\mathcal{S}}(\Phi(s_1),\Phi(s_2)) &=
\max\Bigl\{ d_B(F_c(L_1),F_c(L_2)),\; d_F(F_m(M_1),F_m(M_2)),\; d_{\text{spec}}(F_f(\theta_1),F_f(\theta_2)) \Bigr\}\\
&\le \max\Bigl\{ \beta\,\|L_1-L_2\|_F,\; \alpha\, d_B(M_1,M_2),\; \gamma\, d_F(\theta_1,\theta_2) \Bigr\}\\
&\le \max\{\alpha,\gamma,\beta\}\;
\max\bigl\{ d_B(M_1,M_2),\,d_F(\theta_1,\theta_2),\,\|L_1-L_2\|_F \bigr\}\\
&=\lambda\, d_{\mathcal{S}}(s_1,s_2).
\end{aligned}

Since \lambda<1, \Phi is a contraction. ∎

Theorem C.3 (Unique Cognitive Fixed Point). The map \Phi has a unique fixed point s^*\in\mathcal{S}. This s^* is the societal cognitive equilibrium.

Proof. \mathcal{S} is a complete metric space (Theorem A.9) and \Phi is a contraction (Lemma C.2). The Banach fixed‑point theorem gives the unique fixed point. ∎

