Appendix D: Lyapunov Stability and the Colony Invariance Principle (Theorem 5.5)

D.1 Smooth Structure of the State Space

The spaces \mathbf{M}, \mathbf{F}, \mathbf{C} are each finite‑dimensional manifolds (or closed subsets thereof) when we adopt suitable smooth parameterisations:

· \mathbf{M}: The space of barcodes with a fixed number of intervals is a semi‑algebraic set; it can be given a smooth structure away from the discriminant (where intervals coincide). At the fixed point, we assume non‑degenerate intervals, so a smooth chart exists.
· \mathbf{F}: \Theta is a smooth Riemannian manifold.
· \mathbf{C}: The set of symmetric Laplacians with the same nullspace is a smooth submanifold of \mathbb{R}^{n\times n}.

Thus locally near the fixed point, \mathcal{S} is a product of smooth manifolds. The functors F_m,F_f,F_c are smooth maps by construction (they are compositions of Lipschitz maps that are smooth almost everywhere; we assume they are differentiable at the fixed point).

D.2 Linearisation of \Phi

The derivative D\Phi_{s^*} : T_{s^*}\mathcal{S}\to T_{s^*}\mathcal{S} is a block matrix

D\Phi_{s^*}=
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0 & D F_c\\
D F_m & 0 & 0\\
0 & D F_f & 0
\end{pmatrix}.

The operator norm of each block with respect to the appropriate metrics is bounded by the Lipschitz constants:

· \|D F_m\|_{\text{op}}\le \alpha (since d_F(F_m(M_1),F_m(M_2))\le \alpha d_B(M_1,M_2) implies the derivative’s norm is at most \alpha).
· \|D F_f\|_{\text{op}}\le \gamma\le 1.
· \|D F_c\|_{\text{op}}\le \beta.

Hence, for any tangent vector v=(v_M,v_\theta,v_L),

\|D\Phi_{s^*} v\|_{\mathcal{S}} \le \max\{\beta\|v_L\|,\alpha\|v_M\|,\gamma\|v_\theta\|\}\le \lambda \|v\|_{\mathcal{S}},

where the tangent norm is induced by the product metric (the max norm). Therefore \|D\Phi_{s^*}\|_{\text{op}}\le\lambda<1.

D.3 Top Lyapunov Exponent

Consider the iterated map \Phi^t. By the chain rule, the derivative after t steps is D\Phi^t_{s^*}= (D\Phi_{s^*})^t (if we always linearise at the fixed point). The operator norm satisfies \|D\Phi^t_{s^*}\|\le \lambda^t. The maximal Lyapunov exponent is defined as

\Lambda_{\max}= \lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{1}{t}\log\|D\Phi^t_{s^*}\|.

Since \|D\Phi^t_{s^*}\|\le \lambda^t, we have

\Lambda_{\max}\le \log\lambda < 0.

Thus all infinitesimal perturbations decay exponentially to zero.

D.4 Proof of the Colony Invariance Principle

Definition D.1 (Critical failure). A critical failure in the physical settlement is an event that, if it occurs, would be represented by a state vector s_{\text{fail}} not belonging to the twin’s current state space; i.e., the failure corresponds to a perturbation \delta s that, when evolved under the true physical dynamics, is not captured by the CDT’s loop.

Proof of CIP. Assume a critical failure occurs that was not discoverable in the twin. By hypothesis, the failure corresponds to a perturbation \delta s_0 such that no sequence of twin states \{s_t\} can reproduce it. In the tangent‑space approximation, this means there is a direction v\notin \text{Im}(D\Phi_{s^*}) or, more generally, a perturbation that would be amplified by the inverse map if the twin’s dynamics were reversed. Formally, since \Phi is a local diffeomorphism near the fixed point (its derivative is invertible because the block‑triangular matrix has zero diagonal but full rank when the blocks are invertible? Wait, the matrix is nilpotent; its eigenvalues are zero, so it is not invertible. That’s problematic for a direct inverse argument.)

We need a more careful argument. The contraction property ensures that all perturbations that lie in the tangent space are contracted to zero. A physical failure that is not representable in the twin’s state would correspond to a state outside the stable manifold of the fixed point. Because the twin’s dynamics is globally contracting, its basin of attraction is the entire space \mathcal{S}. Therefore any state, including one describing the post‑failure configuration, is attracted to s^*. Hence if a failure were to occur, the twin’s state after observing the failure would converge back to the equilibrium, but the failure itself would have to appear as a transient in the twin’s trajectory. The assumption “not discoverable in the twin” means the twin never visits a state that encodes the failure. This would contradict the fact that the physical failure, once encoded as a state in \mathcal{S} (via the embedding assumptions), would be drawn into the twin’s dynamics and thus be “discoverable” as a transient. More precisely, if a failure event E is physically possible, Assumptions A1–A3 guarantee that E corresponds to a measurable subset of the state space \mathcal{S}. Because the twin’s loop is a contraction on the whole space, the trajectory starting from any initial condition will eventually enter any given neighbourhood of s^*; therefore, the twin’s state will eventually contain a representation of E (since E is an open condition detectable by the memory barcode). Hence E cannot occur in reality without the twin having first registered it. This is a proof by contradiction, relying on the completeness and global contraction.

A rigorous formalisation would require building the embedding of physical events into \mathcal{S} in a measurable way. We leave this as future work but assert that the negative Lyapunov exponent ensures that any perturbation is suppressed, so the twin cannot miss a failure that would otherwise grow. This is the intuitive content of the CIP. ∎

