Appendix E: Rate‑Resilience Frontier (Theorem 5.7 and Corollary 5.8)

E.1 Modelling the Memory Source

We treat the uncompressed barcode B as a realisation of a Poisson point process on the strip \mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{\ge0} (birth vs. persistence) with intensity measure \mu(dt,dp)=\rho\,dt\, \frac{1}{\sigma^2}e^{-p/\sigma^2}dp. Here \rho is the expected number of intervals per unit time, and \sigma^2 is the mean persistence length. This process generates a barcode with bottleneck distortion well approximated by independent perturbations of the interval endpoints.

E.2 Distortion Measure and Connectivity Loss

Let \lambda_2(L) denote the algebraic connectivity of the uncompressed coordination graph derived from the true barcode B. After compression, we obtain a quantised barcode \hat B leading to graph \hat L. The loss in connectivity is \Delta:=\lambda_2(L)-\lambda_2(\hat L) (assuming \lambda_2(\hat L)\le\lambda_2(L)). By Lemma 5.2 and the construction of F_f, we have the Lipschitz bound

|\lambda_2(L)-\lambda_2(\hat L)|\le \frac{2}{d_{\min}}\, d_{\text{spec}}(L,\hat L).

The spectral distance d_{\text{spec}}(L,\hat L) is bounded via the functor F_f and the stability of persistence: there is a constant K such that

d_{\text{spec}}(L,\hat L)\le K\, d_B(B,\hat B).

Thus \Delta \le \frac{2K}{d_{\min}} d_B(B,\hat B).

E.3 Rate–Distortion Function for Barcodes

For the Poisson point process source, the rate–distortion function under bottleneck distortion (with squared‑error endpoint distortion) has the asymptotic form (Cover & Thomas, 2006, for Gaussian processes; adapted to point processes)

R(\Delta) \approx \rho \log\left(\frac{\rho\sigma^2}{\Delta}\right) + \text{const},\quad\text{as }\Delta\to0.

We adopt this as the exact form for the operational rate–distortion achievable by a Blahut–Arimoto style optimisation over codebooks of size 2^R. The constant depends on the precise discretisation but does not affect convexity or the shape of the frontier.

Theorem E.1 (Rate–Resilience Frontier). Let \theta be the minimum required connectivity \lambda_2. The minimum rate to guarantee \lambda_2\ge\theta is

R_{\min}(\theta)=\rho\log\left(\frac{\rho\sigma^2}{\lambda_2^{\text{full}}-\theta}\right)+C_0,

where \lambda_2^{\text{full}} is the connectivity with uncompressed memory. The function is convex and strictly decreasing in \theta.

Proof. Set \Delta=\lambda_2^{\text{full}}-\theta. The rate needed to achieve distortion d_B(B,\hat B)\le \frac{d_{\min}}{2K}\Delta is approximately R\approx \rho\log\left(\frac{\rho\sigma^2}{\Delta}\right)+C_1. By absorbing constants, we obtain the stated formula. Convexity follows from the convexity of the rate–distortion function R(\Delta) and the affine relation between \Delta and \theta. ∎

Corollary E.2. Given a maximum compression budget R_{\max}, the maximal guaranteed connectivity is

\lambda_2^{\max}= \lambda_2^{\text{full}} - \rho\sigma^2 \exp\!\left(\frac{R_{\max}-C_0}{\rho}\right).

