Appendix F: Computational Complexity of Foresight Coverage (Theorem 5.9)

F.1 Problem Formalisation

Foresight Coverage Problem (FCP)

· Input: A memory barcode M\in\mathbf{M}, a forecast model F (induced by F_m), a set \mathcal{C}=\{c_1,\dots,c_k\} of “catastrophic futures” (each is a measurable subset of the trajectory space), and a threshold \tau\in[0,1].
· Question: Does there exist an action profile (coordination graph) A\in\mathbf{C} such that, under the foresight distribution \mathbb{P}_F, the probability that the selected action covers all catastrophes (i.e., avoids each c_i with sufficient margin) is at least \tau?

We assume \mathcal{C} is represented as a set of intervals (or regions) in the barcode space, so that a memory feature “covers” a catastrophe if its persistence interval intersects the region.

F.2 NP‑Completeness

Theorem F.1. FCP is NP‑complete.

Proof. Membership in NP: Given a candidate action graph A, we can sample from the foresight distribution to estimate coverage probability; the decision can be certified by a sufficiently large sample (with high confidence). Alternatively, if the probability can be computed analytically from the barcode, the verification is polynomial time.

NP‑hardness: We reduce from Set Cover. Let U=\{1,\dots,m\} be a universe, and \mathcal{S}=\{S_1,\dots,S_n\} a collection of subsets. The Set Cover problem asks if there exist k subsets whose union is U.

Construct an instance of FCP as follows:

· Each element j\in U corresponds to a catastrophic mode c_j.
· The memory barcode contains one interval for each subset S_i. The interval’s birth and death are chosen so that it “covers” exactly the catastrophes j\in S_i (e.g., by placing the interval’s support on those coordinates).
· The action profile selects a set of at most k memory intervals (by constructing a coordination graph with exactly those active features).
· Coverage probability \tau is set to 1 (deterministic coverage).

Then a set cover of size k exists iff there is an action covering all catastrophes with probability 1. The reduction is polynomial. Thus FCP is NP‑complete. ∎

F.3 PTAS under Low‑Dimensional Geometry

Assumption. The barcode intervals lie in a metric space of doubling dimension d. In particular, the set of all possible interval endpoints lives in a space where any ball can be covered by 2^{O(d)} balls of half radius. This is guaranteed by A1 (compact metric space with finite Assouad dimension).

Theorem F.2. Under the doubling‑dimension assumption, FCP admits a polynomial‑time approximation scheme (PTAS): for any \varepsilon>0, one can find an action whose coverage probability is at least (1-\varepsilon)\tau^* in time O(n^{\text{poly}(1/\varepsilon)}), where \tau^* is the optimal coverage.

Proof sketch. We discretise the space of catastrophe modes using an \varepsilon-net. The coverage condition becomes a geometric set cover problem where each memory interval corresponds to a ball (or a region) and the goal is to cover a set of points (the catastrophes). Because the metric space has bounded doubling dimension, the number of distinct shapes an interval can take with respect to the net is bounded by n^{O(1/\varepsilon^{d})}. By dynamic programming on the net (or using the shifting technique of Hochbaum–Maass), we can approximate the optimal cover to within 1-\varepsilon. The running time is exponential in 1/\varepsilon but polynomial in n. Hence a PTAS. ∎

