Appendix G: Multi‑Settlement Network Stability (Theorem 5.10)

G.1 Coupled CDT Model

Consider k settlements. Settlement i has its own state space \mathcal{S}_i and local loop \Phi_i:\mathcal{S}_i\to\mathcal{S}_i, which is a contraction with constant \lambda_i<1. In addition, there are inter‑settlement influence functors \Psi_{ij}:\mathcal{S}_j\to\mathcal{S}_i (for i\neq j) that are Lipschitz with constant \varepsilon_{ij}\ge0. The global state is \mathbf{S}=(s_1,\dots,s_k)\in\mathcal{S}^{(k)}:=\prod_{i=1}^k\mathcal{S}_i equipped with the metric

d_{\mathcal{S}^{(k)}}(\mathbf{S},\mathbf{S}')=\max_i\, d_{\mathcal{S}_i}(s_i,s'_i).

The coupled dynamics (in discrete time) is defined by

s_i(t+1)=\Phi_i\bigl(s_i(t)\bigr) \;\oplus\; \sum_{j\neq i} \Psi_{ij}\bigl(s_j(t)\bigr),

where \oplus denotes a weighted average in the tangent space (or a Riemannian mean). To simplify, we assume the interactions are linear in the tangent approximation and the combined map can be written as

\mathbf{S}(t+1)=\mathbf{\Phi}(\mathbf{S}(t)),

with component maps

\bigl[\mathbf{\Phi}(\mathbf{S})\bigr]_i = \frac{1}{1+\sum_{j\neq i}\varepsilon_{ij}}\left( \Phi_i(s_i) + \sum_{j\neq i} \varepsilon_{ij} \Psi_{ij}(s_j) \right).

(The normalisation ensures the new state remains in the appropriate space; exact treatment requires convex combination in a Riemannian sense, but the contraction argument is robust.)

G.2 Contraction Condition

We construct a matrix A\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times k} that captures the Lipschitz amplification:

A_{ij}=
\begin{cases}
\lambda_i/(1+\sum_{\ell\neq i}\varepsilon_{i\ell}), & i=j,\\
\varepsilon_{ij} L_{\Psi_{ij}}/(1+\sum_{\ell\neq i}\varepsilon_{i\ell}), & i\neq j,
\end{cases}

where L_{\Psi_{ij}} is the Lipschitz constant of \Psi_{ij}. Then one can show (by the same product metric reasoning) that

d_{\mathcal{S}^{(k)}}(\mathbf{\Phi}(\mathbf{S}),\mathbf{\Phi}(\mathbf{S}'))\le \|A\|_{\max} \, d_{\mathcal{S}^{(k)}}(\mathbf{S},\mathbf{S}'),

where \|A\|_{\max}=\max_i\sum_j |A_{ij}| is the maximum row sum norm. Consequently, if \|A\|_{\max}<1, \mathbf{\Phi} is a contraction. More generally, we can use any matrix norm; the condition \rho(A)<1 (spectral radius) is sufficient after a suitable choice of product metric (e.g., using a weighted max norm).

Theorem G.1 (Global Contraction). If the spectral radius \rho(A)<1, then \mathbf{\Phi} has a unique global fixed point \mathbf{S}^* and all trajectories converge to it.

Proof. There exists a norm \|\cdot\|_* on \mathbb{R}^k such that the induced operator norm satisfies \|A\|_*<1. Equip \mathcal{S}^{(k)} with the metric d_*(\mathbf{S},\mathbf{S}')=\max_i w_i d_{\mathcal{S}_i}(s_i,s'_i) for suitable weights w_i. Then the Lipschitz constant of \mathbf{\Phi} with respect to d_* is at most \|A\|_*<1. Completeness is inherited, so Banach’s theorem gives the unique fixed point and global convergence. ∎

Thus, as long as the inter‑settlement couplings are small enough that \rho(A)<1, the whole network maintains a stable cognitive equilibrium. The Curator’s Manual includes tuning the \varepsilon_{ij} to satisfy this condition.

