Appendix H: The Curator’s Manual – Complete Set of Constraints

From the above proofs we extract the exact algebraic constraints that the Curator must enforce:

1. Contraction factor: Choose learning rate \eta < 1/(\kappa C_{\text{mean}}C_{\text{stab}}) so that \alpha<1.
2. Graph regularity: Maintain minimum vertex degree d_{\min} and spectral gap \delta_0 such that the Lipschitz constant \gamma\le 1.
3. Compression rate: Set R>0 (in bits per epoch) so that \beta = e^{-R/\rho} + \varepsilon<1. In practice \varepsilon is negligible; any R>0 suffices.
4. Global product: Ensure \lambda=\max\{\alpha,\gamma,\beta\}<1.
5. Rate–Resilience: Given hardware R_{\max}, the Curator must accept a connectivity loss bounded by \lambda_2^{\text{full}}-\lambda_2^{\max} as in Corollary E.2; operating below the frontier risks violating the contraction (if \gamma becomes >1 due to poor clustering).
6. Coverage verification: Use the PTAS with an acceptable error tolerance \varepsilon; exact solving is NP‑complete and should be avoided for large instances.
7. Multi‑settlement: Compute the interaction matrix A and verify \rho(A)<1; if not, reduce coupling strengths \varepsilon_{ij}.

All constraints are algebraic and can be checked without simulation, fulfilling the purely mathematical mandate of the dissertation.

